and we actually don't
know that's the case,

00:45:23.650 --> 00:45:25.980
'cause we don't actually know
definitively, historically,

00:45:25.980 --> 00:45:27.160
the circumstances of their deaths.

00:45:27.160 --> 00:45:28.600
So anyway, but--

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:29.930
I would really like to know though.

00:45:29.930 --> 00:45:32.470
It'd be better than talking
about it like we are right now.

00:45:32.470 --> 00:45:33.303
Right.

00:45:33.303 --> 00:45:34.310
Hey, I was there.

00:45:34.310 --> 00:45:35.940
Right, but I think for
me, it would be like--

00:45:35.940 --> 00:45:38.640
The thing is there were people
who say they were there,

00:45:38.640 --> 00:45:40.190
and then, you know, it's--

00:45:40.190 --> 00:45:41.270
Right, right, but I guess
what I'm saying is--

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:43.230
But it's not definitive.

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:44.063
It's frustrating.

00:45:44.063 --> 00:45:46.720
For me, it would be
there's just like, okay,

00:45:46.720 --> 00:45:49.860
and I'm not saying that,
legitimately, I do not sit up

00:45:49.860 --> 00:45:52.663
at night, I don't think about
this on a regular basis,

00:45:54.411 --> 00:45:55.410
but it was when that question
was asked, it was like,

00:45:55.410 --> 00:45:57.250
you know, let's scratch that off the list,

00:45:57.250 --> 00:46:01.260
because not just for me
personally, but again,

00:46:01.260 --> 00:46:02.467
it's not like I'd come back and say,

00:46:02.467 --> 00:46:04.519
"Hey guys, it didn't happen,

00:46:04.519 --> 00:46:05.530
"I went into the bubble
and it didn't happen."

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:06.363
All these people would be like,

00:46:06.363 --> 00:46:07.350
"We're not Christians anymore."

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:08.900
Like, it doesn't work that way.

00:46:09.860 --> 00:46:12.405
But it was this thing like,
okay, that must still mean

00:46:12.405 --> 00:46:15.270
more to me than I even realize, right?

00:46:15.270 --> 00:46:19.310
The need to want to confirm
the decision that I've made,

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:22.630
to come to the conclusion
that it didn't happen.

00:46:22.630 --> 00:46:24.115
Yeah, definitely.

00:46:24.115 --> 00:46:26.330
I think a part of this
is also, I don't know if,

00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:28.087
did you end up reading

00:46:28.087 --> 00:46:30.650
"The Universal Christ" by Richard Rohr?

00:46:30.650 --> 00:46:31.850
No, not yet, at least.

00:46:34.199 --> 00:46:37.110
So, Richard Rohr, who's a Franciscan Monk,

00:46:38.742 --> 00:46:40.842
who's written a bunch of incredible books,

00:46:42.180 --> 00:46:44.410
from a very different
perspective on Christianity

00:46:44.410 --> 00:46:46.463
than the one we, kind of, came up with.

00:46:48.874 --> 00:46:51.467
And it's definitely a
much more palatable form

00:46:54.210 --> 00:46:55.820
of Christianity for me personally.

00:46:55.820 --> 00:47:00.660
But, I really like the way
he thinks about the idea

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:03.560
of what Jesus is, and
what Jesus represents,

00:47:03.560 --> 00:47:07.223
and what the Christian life is all about.

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:12.430
I mean, so much so that I'm
like, I could get into that.

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:15.360
Like, when I read his
work, that's how I think

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:17.760
'cause it's just like,
it's a way to, kind of,

00:47:17.760 --> 00:47:20.787
capture the hope that I have,

00:47:20.787 --> 00:47:22.680
and also apply it to the tradition

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:26.150
that I still know relatively well,

00:47:26.150 --> 00:47:29.330
but not in a dogmatic way
where it's just like...

00:47:31.320 --> 00:47:33.080
Christianity, for me, most of my life,

00:47:33.080 --> 00:47:38.080
was a set of principles,
basically a set of statements

00:47:40.910 --> 00:47:42.890
that you had to agree with, right?

00:47:42.890 --> 00:47:46.240
And he's like, that's not what it is.

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:47.993
It's basically a way of being.

00:47:49.360 --> 00:47:51.568
Which is an attractive thing.

00:47:51.568 --> 00:47:55.286
But he talks, he believes
in the resurrection.

00:47:55.286 --> 00:47:56.820
He actually talks about it
in "The Universal Christ"

00:47:56.820 --> 00:48:01.500
and talks about the
moment of resurrection,

00:48:01.500 --> 00:48:04.200
he anticipates it would
be like you would see

00:48:04.200 --> 00:48:08.509
light pouring out of the tomb.

00:48:08.509 --> 00:48:13.430
I haven't read it recently,
so I'm not getting this right.

00:48:13.430 --> 00:48:16.480
But the idea that it was
like a dude walking out

00:48:16.480 --> 00:48:18.660
and talking to people,
and I'm not sure exactly

00:48:18.660 --> 00:48:20.130
what he believes, but
he's basically saying

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:23.900
that this is like a cosmic
event that represents something

00:48:23.900 --> 00:48:26.660
much bigger than traditionally
we've given it credit for.

00:48:26.660 --> 00:48:29.530
So I think that it was, kind
of, on my mind a little bit.

00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:32.750
It's like, okay, if I go back
and see what I'm expecting,

00:48:32.750 --> 00:48:37.750
which is, you know, some
natural explanation,

00:48:37.779 --> 00:48:42.779
body stolen, it wasn't even as eventful.

00:48:43.870 --> 00:48:47.410
Maybe his body was consumed by animals,

00:48:47.410 --> 00:48:50.060
which often happened to
people who were crucified.

00:48:50.060 --> 00:48:51.703
Lots of different explanations.

00:48:52.701 --> 00:48:57.701
But if I went back and I saw
the light, or I saw him emerge.

00:49:00.710 --> 00:49:02.380
Or even just an angel.

00:49:02.380 --> 00:49:04.200
If it's like, hey you just missed it,

00:49:04.200 --> 00:49:06.270
like everybody else, by the way.

00:49:06.270 --> 00:49:07.475
Yeah.

00:49:07.475 --> 00:49:12.200
I'm sure it can be seen
as a beautiful thing,

00:49:12.200 --> 00:49:15.160
but to me, it's a frustrating thing.

00:49:15.160 --> 00:49:19.803
It's like, something so
momentous, no one saw it.

00:49:20.840 --> 00:49:25.840
I mean, Jesus appeared
to people afterward.

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:30.800
According to Paul, up to 500 at one time.

00:49:30.800 --> 00:49:32.900
But it was a small movement, man.

00:49:32.900 --> 00:49:33.950
And it wasn't, you know?

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:37.107
It's frustrating.

00:49:37.107 --> 00:49:40.020
Well, again, I think knowing--

00:49:40.020 --> 00:49:40.853
I don't want to--

00:49:40.853 --> 00:49:43.156
But knowing what we know
about the way people work,

00:49:43.156 --> 00:49:46.693
and the way people
begin to believe things,

00:49:47.574 --> 00:49:49.670
and the people's disposition
to believe things,

00:49:49.670 --> 00:49:54.670
it is not unbelievable to
me at all that there could

00:49:56.388 --> 00:49:58.490
have been absolutely
nothing that happened,

00:49:58.490 --> 00:50:00.070
and still the movement would have started.

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.450
I know people say that you
can't explain the beginning

00:50:02.450 --> 00:50:03.970
of Christianity without the resurrection,

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:05.270
I just disagree with that.

00:50:06.790 --> 00:50:09.310
But, I think that if I saw it--

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:11.150
Because look at the other
movements that started.

00:50:11.150 --> 00:50:15.563
Look at all types of
movements, big and small.

00:50:17.660 --> 00:50:21.920
But I think that the idea...
Because I think the question

00:50:21.920 --> 00:50:23.630
is if I were to see something,
if I were to see something

00:50:23.630 --> 00:50:25.360
that was not what I was expecting,

00:50:25.360 --> 00:50:28.280
but it was the resurrection in some form,

00:50:28.280 --> 00:50:30.573
like, what would that do to me, right?

00:50:31.960 --> 00:50:33.440
Would I all of a sudden be like, okay,

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:35.640
I take back everything I said?

00:50:35.640 --> 00:50:37.820
I take back all the issues

00:50:37.820 --> 00:50:41.220
that I have with the historical claims.

00:50:41.220 --> 00:50:42.410
I take back all the issues

00:50:42.410 --> 00:50:45.730
that I have about the scientific stuff.

00:50:45.730 --> 00:50:47.010
Archeological stuff.

00:50:47.010 --> 00:50:50.123
No, I don't think that I
would, but I would be like,

00:50:51.799 --> 00:50:56.440
oh, okay, there is something
here that's very significant

00:50:56.440 --> 00:50:59.350
and might be the reason
that we're all here.

00:50:59.350 --> 00:51:01.403
And it's got something to do with Jesus.

00:51:03.640 --> 00:51:05.650
And again, there are
plenty, I think Richard Rohr

00:51:05.650 --> 00:51:08.513
is an example of it, there
are plenty of Christians

00:51:08.513 --> 00:51:10.870
who don't get hung up on those things

00:51:10.870 --> 00:51:13.490
that I would have had an issue
with and were the reasons

00:51:13.490 --> 00:51:18.490
that I left, who kind of
hold to just the core tenet

00:51:20.380 --> 00:51:25.380
that Jesus represents this,
you know, restoration of God,

00:51:29.200 --> 00:51:31.660
you know, reconciliation
of God and people,

00:51:31.660 --> 00:51:34.770
that broken relationship being healed.

00:51:34.770 --> 00:51:37.590
But I kind of, there's
a part of me that is,

00:51:37.590 --> 00:51:40.630
I don't dispute that anyway.

00:51:40.630 --> 00:51:41.463
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:51:41.463 --> 00:51:44.830
Like, that's cool, I'm okay with that.

00:51:44.830 --> 00:51:49.830
I may not be personally
practicing it or experiencing it,

00:51:50.110 --> 00:51:52.880
but I don't have a problem with that idea.

00:51:52.880 --> 00:51:54.320
I don't have a problem
with any of the stuff

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:56.924
that Richard Rohr said in
his book, in other words.

00:51:56.924 --> 00:52:01.710
I would like to think that
I am open, in the same way

00:52:01.710 --> 00:52:04.592
that if I went back, if
I could do this exercise,

00:52:04.592 --> 00:52:09.592
that I would be open to whatever

00:52:10.035 --> 00:52:12.885
on the other side of that time
warp that I would witness.

00:52:15.320 --> 00:52:17.590
I would try to be as open as possible.

00:52:17.590 --> 00:52:19.480
I try to be as open as possible now.

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:23.950
Well, I mean, our best
friend, Mike McHargue,

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:27.270
wrote a book about this,
in a lot of ways, right?

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:28.678
Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:52:28.678 --> 00:52:30.890
So, he was an evangelical
Christian, he became an atheist

00:52:30.890 --> 00:52:35.423
because of, kind of, studying,
reading a lot, and then--

00:52:35.423 --> 00:52:37.550
It's called "Finding God in the Waves"

00:52:37.550 --> 00:52:38.640
if you're interested.

00:52:38.640 --> 00:52:43.363
And then he had this supernatural
experience with Jesus,

00:52:44.510 --> 00:52:48.130
and so, he would still
call himself a Christian,

00:52:48.130 --> 00:52:53.130
but does he hold to the
evangelical tenets of the,

00:52:53.250 --> 00:52:56.380
not even close, but he holds to the idea

00:52:56.380 --> 00:53:00.430
that there's a loving God with
a purpose in this world and--

00:53:00.430 --> 00:53:02.300
Jesus doesn't make you an evangelical.

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:06.251
Yeah, and Jesus is a central
part of that for him.

00:53:06.251 --> 00:53:09.053
And again, he's way smarter than I am,

00:53:10.070 --> 00:53:11.490
and he's a close friend,

00:53:11.490 --> 00:53:14.420
and I'm not gonna dispute his experience.

00:53:14.420 --> 00:53:15.253
But for me--

00:53:15.253 --> 00:53:17.070
But it was a mystical encounter.

00:53:17.070 --> 00:53:21.590
So, I feel like if I had,
and I continue to be open

00:53:21.590 --> 00:53:23.990
to that kind of encounter,
I mean, just a second ago

00:53:23.990 --> 00:53:25.990
I was talking about potentially
connecting with aliens,

00:53:25.990 --> 00:53:27.340
so I'm open to quite a bit.

00:53:28.370 --> 00:53:32.887
But I think it would just,
it would give me a little bit

00:53:32.887 --> 00:53:37.790
of motivation to move in
a particular direction

00:53:37.790 --> 00:53:39.423
from a spiritual standpoint.

00:53:41.320 --> 00:53:42.250
That's what it would do.

00:53:42.250 --> 00:53:43.967
I've got one more .

00:53:45.320 --> 00:53:48.090
'Cause, I mean, we could
talk about this all day.

00:53:48.090 --> 00:53:48.923
Right.

00:53:50.130 --> 00:53:51.673
But to complete my
exercise, I was like, man,

00:53:51.673 --> 00:53:54.160
I've gotta swing the
pendulum a little bit.

00:53:54.160 --> 00:53:56.820
I mean, what would just be fun?

00:53:56.820 --> 00:53:59.760
Like, I'd like to have
one that was just like,

00:53:59.760 --> 00:54:03.283
I mean, what would I enjoy
the most going back to?

00:54:04.180 --> 00:54:06.720
And, I was like, it probably
has something to do with music.

00:54:06.720 --> 00:54:08.660
That would be cool to witness--

00:54:08.660 --> 00:54:10.120
The first music.

00:54:10.120 --> 00:54:12.377
The first caveman to go like, .

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:18.173
What is happening?

00:54:19.550 --> 00:54:20.703
I think I'm singing.

00:54:21.960 --> 00:54:25.380
Do you think the first song
came before the first word?

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:26.557
Was it like ?

00:54:33.866 --> 00:54:34.699
Yeah, that was it.

00:54:34.699 --> 00:54:36.563
What does "Smoke on the
Water" have at the start?

00:54:36.563 --> 00:54:39.625
.

00:54:43.078 --> 00:54:45.420
Do you think "Smoke on the
Water" was made by a caveman

00:54:45.420 --> 00:54:46.900
who couldn't speak?

00:54:46.900 --> 00:54:50.333
I believe, it sounds, it's
the most caveman-like riff.

00:54:52.870 --> 00:54:56.033
I don't think, that's not
my answer, is what I'm.

00:54:56.033 --> 00:54:59.470
It's not, I quickly moved on
from any of those thoughts,

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:03.540
into, I mean, it'd be
cool to go to Woodstock

00:55:03.540 --> 00:55:07.690
or like, The Last Waltz,
The Band's last concert.

00:55:07.690 --> 00:55:10.830
I mean, then I'm like, I'm
not much of a concert guy.

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:14.160
I mean, the Beatles last
performance on the rooftop

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:18.563
of the Apple building.

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:23.960
Man, that would have been
cool, but I'm not that big

00:55:23.960 --> 00:55:25.253
of a Beatles fan.

00:55:27.330 --> 00:55:29.330
I was like, I'll probably have to go

00:55:29.330 --> 00:55:30.340
with the Merle Haggard thing.

00:55:30.340 --> 00:55:33.490
Okay, can I guess what you went with?

00:55:33.490 --> 00:55:35.950
Yes, 'cause you're going to be wrong.

00:55:35.950 --> 00:55:38.030
Well, I thought you were
gonna say when Johnny Cash

00:55:38.030 --> 00:55:42.190
performed in San Quentin and
Merle Haggard was in the crowd.

00:55:42.190 --> 00:55:43.663
Oh, that would be a good one.

00:55:43.663 --> 00:55:48.663
So, then I'd be, oh, that would be cool.

00:55:49.720 --> 00:55:50.560
I didn't even think of that.

00:55:50.560 --> 00:55:51.393
That's a good one.

00:55:51.393 --> 00:55:53.370
Or you'd be a level above Merle.

00:55:53.370 --> 00:55:55.895
And I'd have to find Merle in there.

00:55:55.895 --> 00:55:56.759
You could find him.

00:55:56.759 --> 00:55:57.903
That'd be really cool.

00:56:01.160 --> 00:56:05.090
I actually settled on
the recording session

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:07.755
for my favorite Merle song.

00:56:07.755 --> 00:56:10.300
"I'm Always on a Mountain When I Fall"

00:56:10.300 --> 00:56:12.180
is my favorite Merle song,

00:56:12.180 --> 00:56:15.120
which incidentally, he did not write.

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:18.270
So, that kind of makes
me wanna change my mind.

00:56:18.270 --> 00:56:21.584
You know, the album from 1978,

00:56:21.584 --> 00:56:26.584
"I'm Always on a Mountain When
I Fall" also the title track,

00:56:27.480 --> 00:56:30.810
which is my favorite, "It's
Been a Great Afternoon"

00:56:30.810 --> 00:56:35.410
is the second track, which is
maybe my second Merle song.

00:56:35.410 --> 00:56:37.750
Talking about having a hangover

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:41.330
and then having an afternoon
delight to get over it.

00:56:41.330 --> 00:56:43.890
It's like, you can write this stuff

00:56:43.890 --> 00:56:48.460
and it be on country radio in 1978.

00:56:48.460 --> 00:56:50.840
So, I would travel back
to the year I was born

00:56:50.840 --> 00:56:53.190
and instead of going to the
concert, I would go to the,

00:56:53.190 --> 00:56:57.330
I would love to be hanging
out in the recording session

00:56:57.330 --> 00:57:00.140
because I love those songs because,

00:57:00.140 --> 00:57:02.780
those two songs in particular,
they represent my favorite

00:57:02.780 --> 00:57:05.960
Merle songs because of the
production and the decisions

00:57:05.960 --> 00:57:08.080
that are made, and you
know, the musicianship.

00:57:08.080 --> 00:57:13.080
And, I mean, someone like
Britain, I interact with him

00:57:16.520 --> 00:57:19.950
with music a lot, and
he's so into live music,

00:57:19.950 --> 00:57:22.440
and he's connected with
that, but that's something

00:57:22.440 --> 00:57:24.890
that we never really
did that much, you know?

00:57:24.890 --> 00:57:28.400
I feel like we missed out on that,

00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:30.830
the way that he talks about it.

00:57:30.830 --> 00:57:33.550
But there is something
magical about being attached

00:57:33.550 --> 00:57:37.400
to a song, a particular
performance of a song,

00:57:37.400 --> 00:57:39.240
that was laid down and was,

00:57:39.240 --> 00:57:42.760
let's say it was the most
like he wanted it to be.

00:57:42.760 --> 00:57:44.660
So, it's like, every pluck of the string,

00:57:44.660 --> 00:57:49.180
or every hit of the hand on the lap,

00:57:49.180 --> 00:57:51.430
you know how on "It's
Been A Great Afternoon"

00:57:51.430 --> 00:57:54.700
the drumbeat is just a guy
playing his lap with his hands?

00:57:54.700 --> 00:57:56.483
It's just so creative.

00:57:57.920 --> 00:58:00.190
You feel like you're in the room,

00:58:00.190 --> 00:58:04.510
and I would love to be in the
room and see that's happening.

00:58:04.510 --> 00:58:07.650
Because every time you listen
to the song, it's like,

00:58:07.650 --> 00:58:09.930
I was there the one time

00:58:09.930 --> 00:58:13.370
that they laid down this
track forever, you know?

00:58:13.370 --> 00:58:14.280
I was in a bubble.

00:58:14.280 --> 00:58:16.320
I was in a bubble, I was in
a safe bubble in the corner.

00:58:16.320 --> 00:58:17.920
They didn't know I was there.

00:58:17.920 --> 00:58:19.823
No, I would wanna talk to them.

00:58:21.130 --> 00:58:21.970
Just hanging out.

00:58:21.970 --> 00:58:23.599
I think in my old age

00:58:23.599 --> 00:58:25.610
I just wanna hang out
in recording studios.

00:58:25.610 --> 00:58:27.970
Maybe I'll be, like, a
recording studio janitor

00:58:27.970 --> 00:58:29.680
slash sound engineer.

00:58:29.680 --> 00:58:31.200
Recording studios are
going away though, man.

00:58:31.200 --> 00:58:32.430
People are just doing
it in their bedrooms.

00:58:32.430 --> 00:58:34.215
Yeah, that's another reason.

00:58:34.215 --> 00:58:35.660
You'd be just a person
in someone's bedroom

00:58:35.660 --> 00:58:36.530
which is weird.

00:58:36.530 --> 00:58:41.277
So, I would go back to
1978, Merle's first album

00:58:41.277 --> 00:58:44.380
when he left Capitol and he went to MCA.

00:58:44.380 --> 00:58:47.020
He was kind of faltering a little bit.

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:48.540
He wanted to try something new.

